{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Oregon's experiment with decriminalizing drugs is coming to an end, as a new law will take effect, making it a crime to possess small amounts of hard drugs. This new law gives individuals caught with illicit drugs the option to either face charges or undergo treatment, which includes completing a behavioral health program.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option aims to divert people from the criminal justice system and towards addiction and mental health services. However, opponents argue that this approach may create a patchwork of policies, as counties are encouraged but not required to develop treatment alternatives. This could lead to inconsistencies in how individuals are treated, depending on the county they are in.\n\n*** \nThe criteria for eligibility for treatment are narrow, and individuals must meet specific requirements, such as having no other charges or warrants, and being medically stable. If an individual meets these requirements, officers will connect them with a mobile behavioral health outreach team, which must make contact within 30 minutes.\n\n*** \nThe new law aims to make it easier for police to crack down on drug use in public and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near certain areas. P.P.S The impact of this law will be felt across the state, regardless of age, and will be closely watched by the country, as people go about their daily activities, whether it's going shopping or deciding what to eat, all while speaking their native tongue.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.004175516311079264}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.6931508779525757}, {"token": " experiment", "logprob": -0.3204382658004761}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -2.8967437174287625e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -4.172316494077677e-06}, {"token": "riminal", "